1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,146
Hi, welcome to another
episode of the Earth Docs.

2
00:00:04,098 --> 00:00:07,512
In August 2014 you can stand
where the water used to be,

3
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:11,981
on the eastern lobe of the southern Aral
Sea, and there is nothing under your boots

4
00:00:12,016 --> 00:00:15,175
but pale, cracked plates of clay
running out to the horizon.

5
00:00:15,987 --> 00:00:20,654
According to NASA's Earth Observatory,
that basin went completely dry that month.

6
00:00:21,351 --> 00:00:26,332
Fifty-four years earlier, in 1960, this
was part of the fourth-largest lake on

7
00:00:26,378 --> 00:00:29,130
Earth, and the towns on
this shoreline fished it.

8
00:00:29,826 --> 00:00:33,507
According to NASA's Earth Observatory,
it has since lost ninety percent of its

9
00:00:33,565 --> 00:00:36,839
volume and shrunk to roughly
a tenth of its former size.

10
00:00:37,535 --> 00:00:39,939
You are standing on a seabed
you could once have sailed.

11
00:00:40,751 --> 00:00:42,063
The Aral isn't a sea.

12
00:00:42,307 --> 00:00:47,055
It's a lake with no outlet, fed by two
rivers, the Amu Darya from the south and

13
00:00:47,102 --> 00:00:48,611
the Syr Darya from the north.

14
00:00:49,308 --> 00:00:53,336
Aladin and colleagues date its origin to
roughly seventeen thousand years ago.

15
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:55,879
Water arrives, and water leaves.

16
00:00:56,482 --> 00:00:58,166
But it leaves only as vapour.

17
00:00:58,410 --> 00:01:01,800
That single fact is the hinge
this whole story turns on.

18
00:01:02,496 --> 00:01:04,052
So what happened to the rivers?

19
00:01:04,749 --> 00:01:08,545
In the nineteen fifties, Soviet planners
looked at those two rivers and saw

20
00:01:08,626 --> 00:01:09,973
something other than a sea.

21
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:14,942
They saw cotton, "white gold", and
a desert they intended to farm.

22
00:01:15,639 --> 00:01:16,904
So they took the rivers.

23
00:01:17,601 --> 00:01:20,701
The Kara Kum Canal opened
in nineteen fifty-six.

24
00:01:21,305 --> 00:01:26,204
According to Guy Fipps of Texas A&M
University, that one canal diverted a

25
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:29,281
third of the Amu Darya's
flow in the year it opened,

26
00:01:29,455 --> 00:01:32,706
into newly irrigated land in
Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan.

27
00:01:33,402 --> 00:01:34,738
Not a third of a trickle.

28
00:01:35,248 --> 00:01:37,176
A third of the river that fed the sea.

29
00:01:37,779 --> 00:01:39,382
And the diversion kept growing.

30
00:01:40,078 --> 00:01:45,187
Fipps records that roughly twenty dams
and reservoirs and sixty major irrigation

31
00:01:45,233 --> 00:01:48,147
schemes went up across the basin
over the following decades.

32
00:01:48,844 --> 00:01:53,256
Central Asian irrigation experts quoted
in his work estimate that only twenty to

33
00:01:53,302 --> 00:01:57,482
twenty-five percent of the water pulled
from those rivers ever reached the crops.

34
00:01:58,179 --> 00:02:01,546
The rest leaked from unlined
canals or evaporated on the way.

35
00:02:02,358 --> 00:02:04,042
So the sea's income was cut.

36
00:02:04,738 --> 00:02:07,432
Its outgoings fell too, but never as fast.

37
00:02:08,128 --> 00:02:11,553
Net evaporation declined;
the rivers declined faster.

38
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:16,777
The deficit grew. What happens to
a basin when fewer drops come in,

39
00:02:16,986 --> 00:02:19,367
and every drop that leaves
it leaves as vapour?

40
00:02:20,063 --> 00:02:24,150
Stand on the shore in the nineteen sixties
and you'd see nothing change from one year

41
00:02:24,196 --> 00:02:28,526
to the next. It was the kind of shift a
fisherman measured by where his boat sat

42
00:02:28,573 --> 00:02:33,495
against the dock. But by nineteen seventy,
the level had fallen past the range of

43
00:02:33,588 --> 00:02:36,119
natural variation the sea
had shown in the past.

44
00:02:36,816 --> 00:02:41,750
Here's how we know: hydrologists worked
out the water budget term by term,

45
00:02:42,075 --> 00:02:45,860
river inflow, rainfall,
groundwater, evaporation.

46
00:02:46,557 --> 00:02:51,061
Columbia University's Aral Sea Crisis
project, drawing on Micklin's two thousand

47
00:02:51,131 --> 00:02:53,244
seven analysis, gives the result.

48
00:02:53,941 --> 00:02:57,819
In the first decade of desiccation, the
water level fell by about twenty-one

49
00:02:57,865 --> 00:03:02,068
centimetres a year. In the next
decade, by about fifty-seven.

50
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:04,541
Then it accelerated further.

51
00:03:05,052 --> 00:03:07,385
Why would a shrinking sea shrink faster?

52
00:03:07,768 --> 00:03:09,115
Because it got shallower.

53
00:03:09,556 --> 00:03:12,691
The same sunlight now had to
heat a smaller volume of water,

54
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:16,812
so the surface warmed faster, and
warmer water evaporates faster,

55
00:03:17,021 --> 00:03:18,786
and the level dropped further still.

56
00:03:19,483 --> 00:03:22,931
The Aral was paying for its own
disappearance out of a shrinking account.

57
00:03:23,627 --> 00:03:27,760
Between nineteen sixty and nineteen
ninety, according to Guy Fipps of Texas

58
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:31,766
A&M, the sea lost about six
hundred cubic kilometres of water.

59
00:03:32,462 --> 00:03:36,526
Picture a cube of water roughly eight
and a half kilometres along every edge.

60
00:03:37,223 --> 00:03:38,423
Now picture it gone.

61
00:03:39,150 --> 00:03:43,399
And the water that left took nothing with
it, which raises the question of what it

62
00:03:43,446 --> 00:03:47,509
left behind. Picture the shore of a
fishing town in the late nineteen

63
00:03:47,579 --> 00:03:51,584
seventies. The boats are still
tied up, or run aground,

64
00:03:51,910 --> 00:03:53,431
or sitting upright on sand.

65
00:03:54,244 --> 00:03:58,830
Photographs from the region show exactly
that, trawlers stranded kilometres from

66
00:03:58,899 --> 00:04:02,510
open water. They stopped
sailing because the water left.

67
00:04:03,207 --> 00:04:05,076
But there's a second reason they stayed.

68
00:04:05,679 --> 00:04:10,626
The salt stayed. Every dissolved mineral
the rivers carried in stayed in the basin

69
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:12,321
when the water evaporated off.

70
00:04:13,017 --> 00:04:17,313
Salinity rose from about ten parts per
thousand in nineteen sixty to ninety-two

71
00:04:17,383 --> 00:04:21,898
parts per thousand by two thousand four,
according to Zavialov's measurements cited

72
00:04:21,933 --> 00:04:24,871
in Columbia University's
Aral Sea Crisis project.

73
00:04:25,567 --> 00:04:29,398
The University of West Florida's
environmental science text puts the

74
00:04:29,456 --> 00:04:33,172
southern sea at that point at roughly
three times the salt of seawater.

75
00:04:33,868 --> 00:04:35,076
Fish can't live in that.

76
00:04:35,679 --> 00:04:39,035
According to Bosch, cited in
the same Columbia project,

77
00:04:39,278 --> 00:04:42,494
the commercial fishery that landed
forty-three thousand four hundred and

78
00:04:42,541 --> 00:04:46,883
thirty tons in nineteen sixty landed
nothing at all by nineteen eighty.

79
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:50,621
Twenty years, from a
working industry to zero.

80
00:04:51,434 --> 00:04:55,369
The University of West Florida's text
records twenty-four species of fish in

81
00:04:55,439 --> 00:04:58,969
that water. They could not
adapt to the rising salt,

82
00:04:59,143 --> 00:05:03,324
and they are gone. And the salt
didn't stay in the basin either.

83
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:09,048
NASA describes winds lifting salt-laden
sediment off the exposed seabed and

84
00:05:09,094 --> 00:05:13,761
dropping tens of thousands of tons of it
a year onto farmland and towns downwind.

85
00:05:14,458 --> 00:05:18,672
Fipps notes that dust is likely to
carry pesticide and chemical residues,

86
00:05:18,916 --> 00:05:21,668
and links it to serious health
problems across the region.

87
00:05:22,364 --> 00:05:25,093
The sea had been doing
one other job, quietly.

88
00:05:25,696 --> 00:05:27,542
It moderated the climate of a desert.

89
00:05:28,355 --> 00:05:31,849
The University of West Florida's text
notes that the regional climate is now

90
00:05:31,931 --> 00:05:34,322
drier, with greater temperature extremes.

91
00:05:35,274 --> 00:05:36,474
The lake was weather.

92
00:05:37,341 --> 00:05:40,777
So what happens when a body of
water that size tears itself apart?

93
00:05:41,590 --> 00:05:45,375
By nineteen ninety, the Aral was
no longer one body of water.

94
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:49,601
It had torn in two. According
to Aladin and colleagues,

95
00:05:49,810 --> 00:05:53,212
writing in the journal Zoological
Studies in two thousand twenty-three,

96
00:05:53,595 --> 00:05:57,403
the surface area fell from sixty-seven
thousand four hundred and ninety-nine

97
00:05:57,438 --> 00:06:01,327
square kilometres in nineteen sixty to
thirty-nine thousand seven hundred and

98
00:06:01,374 --> 00:06:02,929
thirty-four by nineteen ninety.

99
00:06:03,626 --> 00:06:06,552
Nearly twenty-eight thousand square
kilometres of water surface,

100
00:06:06,726 --> 00:06:07,926
gone in thirty years.

101
00:06:08,526 --> 00:06:11,266
And the water didn't retreat
to a tidy new shoreline.

102
00:06:11,962 --> 00:06:14,563
According to the University
of West Florida's text,

103
00:06:14,888 --> 00:06:18,905
the present shore sits tens of kilometres
from towns that were once ports.

104
00:06:19,602 --> 00:06:21,076
The sea didn't just leave.

105
00:06:21,517 --> 00:06:24,791
It left a desert between itself and
everyone who used to live on it.

106
00:06:25,488 --> 00:06:28,100
The Amu Darya delta is another casualty.

107
00:06:28,796 --> 00:06:32,825
Fipps records that the delta once covered
five hundred and fifty thousand hectares

108
00:06:32,872 --> 00:06:34,497
of wetland and reed bed.

109
00:06:35,101 --> 00:06:37,167
It has shrunk to less
than twenty thousand.

110
00:06:37,771 --> 00:06:41,916
The shallower the basin, the sooner
a falling level cuts it into pieces.

111
00:06:42,612 --> 00:06:46,083
The northern Small Aral separated
from the southern Large Aral,

112
00:06:46,525 --> 00:06:48,336
each now fed by a different river.

113
00:06:49,032 --> 00:06:53,293
The Large Aral split again, into
an eastern lobe and a western one.

114
00:06:53,897 --> 00:06:57,903
Every fragment got its own depth,
its own salt, its own odds.

115
00:06:58,599 --> 00:07:02,314
In two thousand five, Kazakhstan
finished the Kokaral Dam,

116
00:07:02,523 --> 00:07:05,368
sealing off the northern
sea to hold its water in.

117
00:07:06,064 --> 00:07:09,872
NASA's Earth Observatory reports that
fisheries in the north rebounded.

118
00:07:10,313 --> 00:07:14,969
The same agency's earlier reporting is
blunter: the dam ended any prospect of

119
00:07:15,027 --> 00:07:19,230
recovery for the southern sea, which some
authorities already regarded as beyond

120
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:23,850
help. So one half of the Aral is
recovering and one half is not.

121
00:07:24,547 --> 00:07:26,904
Why would the southern
half fail that completely,

122
00:07:27,113 --> 00:07:28,959
and how far does the failure go?

123
00:07:29,655 --> 00:07:32,790
Hypersalinity doesn't stop at
the point where the fish die.

124
00:07:33,486 --> 00:07:37,086
Aladin and colleagues, writing in
Zoological Studies in two thousand

125
00:07:37,132 --> 00:07:41,451
twenty-three, describe the remnants of the
southern Large Aral as progressing toward

126
00:07:41,486 --> 00:07:46,386
what they call a "Dead Sea" condition,
water hostile to all animal life.

127
00:07:47,082 --> 00:07:49,463
Not depleted. Not degraded.

128
00:07:50,159 --> 00:07:52,899
Finished, for anything
that swims or crawls.

129
00:07:53,596 --> 00:07:55,407
And it isn't a smooth decline.

130
00:07:56,104 --> 00:08:00,515
NASA's Earth Observatory records that the
eastern lobe was completely dry in August

131
00:08:00,539 --> 00:08:01,739
two thousand fourteen.

132
00:08:02,408 --> 00:08:05,879
In twenty seventeen, a wet
year, it partly refilled,

133
00:08:06,123 --> 00:08:10,790
helped by large releases from the Toktogul
reservoir and an unusually warm spring

134
00:08:10,848 --> 00:08:14,041
that melted snow and glacial
ice in the Tien Shan.

135
00:08:14,854 --> 00:08:18,662
But Philip Micklin, the geographer
who has studied this sea for decades,

136
00:08:19,045 --> 00:08:23,236
told NASA those events do not signal a
restoration of the eastern lobe as a

137
00:08:23,317 --> 00:08:27,021
permanent feature. What he
expects is the pattern itself,

138
00:08:27,462 --> 00:08:31,734
refilling in heavy flow years,
near-total dry-out in low-flow years,

139
00:08:31,908 --> 00:08:33,266
for the foreseeable future.

140
00:08:33,592 --> 00:08:37,957
That's where the science is still open:
whether that oscillation is the new steady

141
00:08:38,015 --> 00:08:41,904
state, or the last stage of a one-way
decline with the wet years as

142
00:08:41,974 --> 00:08:45,817
interruptions. The evidence
currently favours Micklin's reading.

143
00:08:46,513 --> 00:08:49,346
What nobody disputes is the
arithmetic that produced it.

144
00:08:49,671 --> 00:08:51,401
The Aral had no outlet.

145
00:08:51,843 --> 00:08:55,999
Every drop that left it left as vapour,
which means every drop of river water

146
00:08:56,045 --> 00:08:59,586
taken upstream was a drop subtracted
from the sea permanently.

147
00:08:59,911 --> 00:09:01,642
Not borrowed. Not delayed.

148
00:09:02,025 --> 00:09:05,310
Subtracted. The Great Salt
Lake has no outlet either.

149
00:09:05,914 --> 00:09:08,910
According to a report by Utah
State University and others,

150
00:09:09,153 --> 00:09:13,704
cited by the US Geological Survey,
consumptive water use has reduced net

151
00:09:13,762 --> 00:09:17,977
river inflow to that lake by thirty-nine
percent over the past one hundred and

152
00:09:18,023 --> 00:09:21,866
fifty years. The Aral isn't a
story about a faraway place.

153
00:09:22,249 --> 00:09:24,803
It's the same sum, running
in a different desert,

154
00:09:25,012 --> 00:09:26,870
and that one, you can drive to.
